<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document>
<name>Hong Kong discovery (2026-12-25 to 2026-12-27)</name>
<Folder><name>Food &amp; Drink</name>
<Placemark><name>Yat Lok Roast Goose</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Family roast-goose shop founded in 1957, now a Michelin-starred counter. Quarter goose over lai fun in broth is the order; char siu and soy chicken also recommended.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Locally beloved siu mei institution; open daily, so workable on both holiday days. Cash only.</p><p>iconic · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Yat%20Lok%20Roast%20Goose%2C%20G/F%2C%20Conwell%20House%2C%2034-38%20Stanley%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.155327,22.282471,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kam&#x27;s Roast Goose</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Third-generation roast goose house with about 30 seats; goose blood pudding and noodles tossed in goose fat are side highlights.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Michelin-starred siu mei with a 70-year family lineage; locally beloved, queues are normal.</p><p>iconic · Wan Chai</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kam%27s%20Roast%20Goose%2C%20G/F%2C%20Po%20Wah%20Commercial%20Centre%2C%20226%20Hennessy%20Road%2C%20Wan%20Chai%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.1753,22.27766,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kau Kee Restaurant</name><description><![CDATA[<p>No-frills beef brisket and tendon noodle house trading since the 1930s, with shared tables and curry or broth options. Milk tea also recommended.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Old-school Central institution; cash only and queues of up to an hour or more.</p><p>iconic · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kau%20Kee%20Restaurant%2C%2021%20Gough%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.152537,22.284229,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lau Sum Kee Noodle (Fuk Wing Street)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Since 1956; third generation still kneads dough with a bamboo pole. Shrimp-roe tossed noodles and prawn wonton noodles are the draws.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Locally beloved Sham Shui Po noodle house with Michelin recognition; a pillar of the bamboo-pole noodle craft.</p><p>strong · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lau%20Sum%20Kee%20Noodle%20%28Fuk%20Wing%20Street%29%2C%2082%20Fuk%20Wing%20Street%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.16303,22.33189,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dragons&#x27; Den</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Retro mid-century dining room in a mall near Lion Rock reviving hard-to-find Cantonese dishes: baked egg omelette with fish tripe, dry-aged pear-wood-smoked squab, dim sum lunch.</p><p><b>Why:</b> One of six new Hong Kong Bib Gourmand entries in the 2026 guide (March 2026); a retro-themed room inside a housing-estate mall.</p><p>strong · Wong Tai Sin</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Dragons%27%20Den%2C%20Shop%20S02%2C%202F%2C%20Tin%20Ma%20Court%20Commercial%20Centre%2C%2055%20Chuk%20Yuen%20Road%2C%20Wong%20Tai%20Sin%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.189427,22.345091,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ho Ho Chak</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Chiu Chow restaurant run by three brothers who also wholesale seafood and fruit. Steamed pork patty with mud crab and fried omelette with dried radish.</p><p><b>Why:</b> New 2026 Bib Gourmand entry; modern-feeling Chiu Chow in TST.</p><p>strong · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Ho%20Ho%20Chak%2C%209F%2C%20Nathan%20Hill%2C%2038%20Hillwood%20Road%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.17399,22.302627,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lai&#x27;s Kitchen</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Dai pai dong-inspired Cantonese room: budget rice and noodle dishes at lunch; wok stir-fries and claypot rice cooked to order at dinner, including three-treasure claypot.</p><p><b>Why:</b> New 2026 Bib Gourmand; part of the Flower Drum group. A tidy way to try winter claypot rice.</p><p>strong · Wan Chai</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lai%27s%20Kitchen%2C%201F%2C%20Harvard%20Commercial%20Building%2C%20105-111%20Thomson%20Road%2C%20Wan%20Chai%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.174121,22.277173,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sing Heung Yuen</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Roadside dai pai dong on Mee Lun Street, open since 1959, known for tomato soup noodles, egg and beef toasted sandwiches and crispy butter-honey bun.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Iconic surviving dai pai dong in Central; appears on Time Out&#x27;s July 2026 dai pai dong list.</p><p>iconic · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sing%20Heung%20Yuen%2C%202%20Mee%20Lun%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.152518,22.283821,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>ChorLand Cookfood Stall</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Dai pai dong-style stall in To Kwa Wan, listed first on Time Out&#x27;s July 2026 dai pai dong list: sweet and sour pork, clams in black bean sauce, salt and pepper corn, pork-lard soy rice.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Newer wave of dai pai dong in the 13 Streets area; wok-hei cooking.</p><p>niche · To Kwa Wan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=ChorLand%20Cookfood%20Stall%2C%20G/F%2C%20Po%20Kwong%20Building%2C%202C%20Ma%20Hang%20Chung%20Road%2C%20To%20Kwa%20Wan%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.188118,22.319759,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Haiphong Road Temporary Cooked Food Hawker Bazaar</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Cluster of cooked-food stalls: satay beef noodles, pork chops, Hainan chicken rice, beef balls.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Cheap, local TST option away from the promenade crowds.</p><p>niche · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Haiphong%20Road%20Temporary%20Cooked%20Food%20Hawker%20Bazaar%2C%20Haiphong%20Road%20Temporary%20Market%2C%20Haiphong%20Road%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.170413,22.298075,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lok Hau Fook</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Chiu Chow restaurant founded 1954 with a dated interior: melon pancake, chicken dumplings, sugar-coated taro. Featured in the film Twilight of the Warriors: Walled In.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Heritage Chiu Chow in the old Walled City neighbourhood; a PolyU Aug 2026 heritage walk stops here, indicating it operates.</p><p>strong · Kowloon City</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lok%20Hau%20Fook%2C%20G/F%2C%201-3%20Hau%20Wong%20Road%2C%20Kowloon%20City%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.18854,22.327886,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kwan Kee Claypot Rice</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Charcoal-fired claypot rice with a crisp scorched base; long waits.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Locally loved winter institution; fits the season.</p><p>strong · Sai Ying Pun</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kwan%20Kee%20Claypot%20Rice%2C%20263%20Queen%27s%20Road%20West%2C%20Sai%20Ying%20Pun%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.143035,22.287066,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sheung Hei Claypot Rice</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Claypot rice specialist formerly Bib Gourmand; lap mei sausage and spare ribs, eel with field chicken. Weekend queues are long.</p><p><b>Why:</b> One of the most-cited winter claypot stops in the west.</p><p>strong · Kennedy Town</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sheung%20Hei%20Claypot%20Rice%2C%2025%20North%20Street%2C%20Kennedy%20Town%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.129251,22.28307,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ser Wong Fun</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Snake soup house founded in Guangzhou in 1895; also chicken and pig-liver-sausage claypot rice.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The classic winter snake soup stop; a 2026 hotel partnership suggests the brand is active.</p><p>strong · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Ser%20Wong%20Fun%2C%2030%20Cochrane%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.154265,22.282818,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Luk Yu Tea House</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Art Deco-era Cantonese teahouse (opened 1933) with wooden booths, ceiling fans and stained glass, serving traditional yum cha and a la carte Cantonese. Michelin&#x27;s blurb singles out deep-fried chicken and fried beef noodles.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The last grand old-style Cantonese teahouse in Central; iconic for atmosphere, waiter-led service and old-school dim sum rather than for value. Locally beloved by regulars; expect a formal, sometimes brusque welcome for walk-ins.</p><p>iconic · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Luk%20Yu%20Tea%20House%2C%2024-26%20Stanley%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.155578,22.282254,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tim Ho Wan (Sham Shui Po)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The Michelin-recognised budget dim sum shop of the chain founded by ex-Four Seasons chefs: baked BBQ pork buns, shrimp dumplings, steamed beef balls with tangerine peel. Opened 2010 in Sham Shui Po.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The Michelin-cheap-dim-sum phenomenon started in this neighbourhood; it has held a Bib Gourmand for 17 straight years (2026 edition) and sits next to Fuk Wing Street&#x27;s toy and wonton-noodle strip. Popular with visitors, so a &#x27;famous but still good value&#x27; pick rather than a hidden one.</p><p>strong · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tim%20Ho%20Wan%20%28Sham%20Shui%20Po%29%2C%209-11%20Fuk%20Wing%20Street%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.161322,22.333071,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mak Man Kee Noodle Shop</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Family wonton-noodle shop (roots in a 1945 food cart): fat shrimp wontons in thin skin, springy duck-egg noodles, rich dried-flounder broth; noodles can be tossed in shrimp roe or oyster sauce.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Locally beloved Bib Gourmand and the benchmark for Cantonese wonton noodles; Michelin lists it open until 00:30, so it doubles as a late bowl. Cash only.</p><p>iconic · Jordan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Mak%20Man%20Kee%20Noodle%20Shop%2C%2051%20Parkes%20Street%2C%20Jordan%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.170533,22.30469,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tsim Chai Kee Noodle (Wellington Street)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Small Wellington Street noodle shop for big fish-ball, beef-ball and shrimp wontons in clear broth with thin egg noodles; a choice of three toppings per bowl. Bib Gourmand in the 2026 Michelin list.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Quick, cheap, Michelin-recognised wonton-noodle stop in the middle of Central; popular with office workers and tourists, so locals&#x27; favourites may be elsewhere. Cash only.</p><p>strong · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tsim%20Chai%20Kee%20Noodle%20%28Wellington%20Street%29%2C%2098%20Wellington%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.154467,22.282937,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hop Yik Tai</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Sham Shui Po street-snack shop famed for freshly steamed, lard-scented rice rolls (cheung fun) with sesame, sweet and chilli sauces, plus fish balls, pork skin and braised radish.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Locally beloved, always-busy cheung fun counter; the classic cheap Sham Shui Po snack. Cash only.</p><p>strong · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hop%20Yik%20Tai%2C%20121%20Kweilin%20Street%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.161181,22.330526,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Trusty Congee King (Wan Chai)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Long-running congee shop cooking its congee in fish broth: pork liver and scallop congee, poached grass-carp skin and sticky-rice dumplings with salted egg yolk are the recommended orders.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Locally reliable congee institution (Bib Gourmand in the 2026 list), open all day and handy as a warm breakfast or late snack on a cold December day.</p><p>strong · Wan Chai</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Trusty%20Congee%20King%20%28Wan%20Chai%29%2C%207%20Heard%20Street%2C%20Wan%20Chai%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.177134,22.277527,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Australia Dairy Company</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Fast-turnover cha chaan teng famed for glossy scrambled eggs on toast, steamed egg custard and milk pudding, plus macaroni soup and milk tea. Waiters are famously brisk and tables are shared.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The canonical &#x27;queue-for-scrambled-eggs&#x27; cha chaan teng; Michelin cites it among the traditional spots keeping the classic offerings alive. Popular with tourists, loved by locals for the speed and ritual.</p><p>iconic · Jordan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Australia%20Dairy%20Company%2C%2047%20Parkes%20Street%2C%20Jordan%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.170594,22.304613,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kam Wah Cafe &amp; Bakery</name><description><![CDATA[<p>1970s cha chaan teng known for its fresh-baked pineapple bun with a slab of butter (bo lo yau), plus egg tarts, French toast and milk tea.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The stop for a proper pineapple bun with butter; locally beloved and heavily visited. A Sept 2026 Taiwanese-press roundup lists it as open 06:30-23:30, so it covers late-night supper too.</p><p>strong · Prince Edward</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kam%20Wah%20Cafe%20%26%20Bakery%2C%20G/F%2C%2047%20Bute%20Street%2C%20Prince%20Edward%20%28Mong%20Kok%29%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.16975,22.322299,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mido Cafe</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A 1950 bing sutt with mosaic floor tiles, wooden booths and arched windows over Temple Street; baked pork chop rice, claypot dishes, milk tea and toasts.</p><p><b>Why:</b> One of the few surviving old-style bing sutt interiors, a retro-HK film-set staple. Food is average; the room is the reason.</p><p>strong · Yau Ma Tei</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Mido%20Cafe%2C%2063%20Temple%20Street%2C%20Yau%20Ma%20Tei%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.170369,22.310226,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tai Cheong Bakery (Lyndhurst Terrace)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Hong Kong&#x27;s most famous egg-tart bakery (from 1954): shortcrust egg tarts with a smooth, wobbly custard, plus butter-crust and milk-white variants and cookies.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The tart reportedly praised by the last governor; the Central flagship near the Mid-Levels Escalator is still listed in 2026 coverage. All Kowloon branches closed by Nov 2024 (about six remain city-wide).</p><p>strong · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tai%20Cheong%20Bakery%20%28Lyndhurst%20Terrace%29%2C%20G/F%2C%2035%20Lyndhurst%20Terrace%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.153578,22.282628,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kai Kai Dessert (Pitt Street, Jordan)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Traditional sweet-soup shop (since the 1970s) known for stone-ground black sesame paste, almond cream and walnut cream, plus mango-pomelo-sago style desserts. The original Ning Po Street shop closed 17 Sep 2025 and relocated to Pitt Street.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Michelin-recognised for nine straight years as a street-food icon (Bib Gourmand in the 2026 list); a locally loved cult sweet-soup stop and open past midnight.</p><p>strong · Jordan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kai%20Kai%20Dessert%20%28Pitt%20Street%2C%20Jordan%29%2C%20G/F%2C%20113-115%20Pitt%20Street%2C%20Jordan%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.169124,22.313647,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Heartwarming (Prince Edward)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Black-sesame specialist with two stone mills grinding at the door: hot sweet black sesame soup, ice cream, pudding and chewy sesame rolls.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A 2025 Bib Gourmand newcomer still listed in 2026; a modern take on the winter sweet soup, near Mong Kok.</p><p>niche · Prince Edward</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Heartwarming%20%28Prince%20Edward%29%2C%2023%20Yu%20Chau%20Street%2C%20Prince%20Edward%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.163363,22.328448,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fong Wing Kee Satay Beef Specialist</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Kowloon City institution (50+ years per Tatler) serving satay-broth hot pot with hand-cut beef and broad noodles, in the neighbourhood&#x27;s Chiu Chow/Thai-influenced satay tradition.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Old-school, locally anchored hot pot for a cold December night, listed in Tatler Asia&#x27;s Dec 2025 hot pot guide; pairs with a Kowloon City Thai crawl.</p><p>niche · Kowloon City</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Fong%20Wing%20Kee%20Satay%20Beef%20Specialist%2C%20G/F%2C%2085-87%20Hau%20Wong%20Road%2C%20Kowloon%20City%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.188781,22.329784,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Chuen Kee Seafood (Hoi Pong Street)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Family-run waterfront seafood restaurant (since the 1980s) with a rooftop terrace by the Sai Kung quay: pick live fish from tanks, cooked as you choose; abalone and salt-and-pepper mantis shrimp are the highlights.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Classic Sai Kung seafood day-out, listed in the 2026 Michelin Guide. Sai Kung is also an easy base for Lei Yue Mun alternatives; busy on holiday weekends.</p><p>strong · Sai Kung</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Chuen%20Kee%20Seafood%20%28Hoi%20Pong%20Street%29%2C%2053%20Hoi%20Pong%20Street%2C%20Sai%20Kung%2C%20New%20Territories%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.272438,22.379761,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hyde Park Garden Seafood Restaurant</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Two-storey Lei Yue Mun seafood house that grew from a dai pai dong. Choose seafood from the owner&#x27;s stall across the way and have it cooked for a fee; Michelin cites razor clams in black bean, fish-and-tofu soup and ginger-scallion abalone claypot.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The Lei Yue Mun pick-and-cook village ritual with a Michelin-listed kitchen; open to 23:00 so it works for a late dinner after Kowloon sightseeing.</p><p>strong · Lei Yue Mun</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hyde%20Park%20Garden%20Seafood%20Restaurant%2C%2044%20Hoi%20Pong%20Road%20Central%2C%20Lei%20Yue%20Mun%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.236495,22.290766,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lung King Heen</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Harbour-view Cantonese restaurant in the Four Seasons, famous for refined dim sum lunch and double-boiled soups; Michelin&#x27;s page shows two stars.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The splurge Cantonese meal: a dim sum lunch here is the established way to do high-end HK dining. Weekend lunch starts 11:30.</p><p>iconic · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lung%20King%20Heen%2C%204/F%2C%20Four%20Seasons%20Hotel%20Hong%20Kong%2C%208%20Finance%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.15892,22.284098,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>BTS Food Station</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Cheap Kowloon City Thai favourite: Hainan chicken rice, crispy pork knuckle, boat noodles, sizzling Thai oyster omelette and tom yum seafood pot.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Kowloon City is HK&#x27;s &#x27;Little Bangkok&#x27; via Thai Teochew immigrants; HK01 (31 Jul 2026) names BTS as one of the few Thai shops open to midnight, so it fits a late dinner.</p><p>niche · Kowloon City</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=BTS%20Food%20Station%2C%2011%20Shing%20Nam%20Road%2C%20Kowloon%20City%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.189736,22.328329,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lee Keung Kee Egg Waffles (North Point)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Original-flavour egg waffle (gai daan jai) shop, crisp outside and soft inside; has since opened extra branches in Tsim Sha Tsui and Po Lam.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A long-standing, locally cited egg waffle benchmark; use it alongside the Quarry Bay / North Point tram walk.</p><p>niche · North Point</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lee%20Keung%20Kee%20Egg%20Waffles%20%28North%20Point%29%2C%20492%20King%27s%20Road%2C%20North%20Point%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.201632,22.291238,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Cafés &amp; Nightlife</name>
<Placemark><name>Bar Leone</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Italian aperitivo-style cocktail bar, known for its martini and fig-leaf negroni. Walk-in only.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Retained Asia&#x27;s top Hong Kong bar and a high world ranking in 2026 (Asia&#x27;s 50 Best Bars No. 3 per Epicure Asia); queue forms early.</p><p>iconic · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Bar%20Leone%2C%2011-15%20Bridges%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.150183,22.283571,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Argo</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Hotel bar led by an extensive Middle East-inflected spirits list; on the 2026 World&#x27;s 50 Best Bars list.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A hotel-bar splurge, open to 1am (2am Fri/Sat) per Time Out and Cathay; in a hotel, so likely open on Christmas.</p><p>strong · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Argo%2C%20G/F%2C%20Four%20Seasons%20Hotel%20Hong%20Kong%2C%208%20Finance%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.15892,22.284098,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>COA</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Agave-spirits bar with 200+ mezcals and tequilas and a 41-page agave menu, down steps off Hollywood Road.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Long-running regional leader (Asia&#x27;s best bar 2021); sustained presence on the 2026 Asia&#x27;s 50 Best list at No. 24.</p><p>strong · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=COA%2C%20Shop%20A%2C%20LG/F%2C%20Wah%20Shin%20House%2C%206-10%20Shin%20Hing%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.152058,22.284027,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Opposites</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Cocktail bar by Antonio Lai and Samuel Kwok whose playful twin-version drinks climbed 26 places to No. 54 on the extended 2026 list.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Fresh Asia&#x27;s 50 Best (51-100) result, July 2026.</p><p>niche · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=The%20Opposites%2C%20LG/F%2C%20Hilltop%20Plaza%2C%2049%20Hollywood%20Road%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.153092,22.282746,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lan Kwai Fong and SoHo</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Hong Kong&#x27;s classic bar-and-club lanes, with SoHo&#x27;s restaurant-bar strip up the Escalator.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Christmas Eve and nights around it are traditionally rowdy street parties here; Christmas night is busy. Official 2026 event details were not found.</p><p>iconic · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lan%20Kwai%20Fong%20and%20SoHo%2C%20Lan%20Kwai%20Fong%2C%20D%27Aguilar%20Street%20area%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.155615,22.280746,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lan Fong Yuen</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Cha chaan teng credited with silk-stocking milk tea (1952) and said to have started yuenyeung; takeout line moves fast.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Origin-story milk tea shop; hours listed Mon-Sat 07:30-18:00 with Sunday closed (possibly outdated).</p><p>strong · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lan%20Fong%20Yuen%2C%202%20Gage%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.1538,22.28264,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>On The Hill Coffee Bar (Tai Kwun)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Local specialty coffee brand roasting its own beans, with a bar inside the Tai Kwun heritage compound (the former Central Police Station).</p><p><b>Why:</b> Home-grown roaster (not a chain import) in a heritage courtyard, convenient for a coffee between Tai Kwun, PMQ and Hollywood Road.</p><p>niche · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=On%20The%20Hill%20Coffee%20Bar%20%28Tai%20Kwun%29%2C%20Tai%20Kwun%2C%2010%20Hollywood%20Road%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.154009,22.281156,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>My Little Coffee Roastery</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Small in-house roastery and coffee counter on Tai Nan Street, on the Sham Shui Po / Prince Edward border.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Local micro-roaster close to the Sham Shui Po fabric and cafe streets; good for a quiet cup away from tourist cafes.</p><p>niche · Prince Edward</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=My%20Little%20Coffee%20Roastery%2C%20G/F%2C%20120%20Tai%20Nan%20Street%2C%20Prince%20Edward%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.165251,22.325858,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Introvert Coffee &amp; Roastery</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Sham Shui Po specialty cafe that roasts its own beans, in the district&#x27;s cafe-and-craft streets.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Indie roaster in Sham Shui Po, easy to combine with the fabric and craft streets and Apliu Street.</p><p>niche · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Introvert%20Coffee%20%26%20Roastery%2C%20Shop%20132A%2C%20Ki%20Lung%20Street%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.161621,22.329126,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>OZONE (Ritz-Carlton Hong Kong)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Bar on the 118th floor of ICC billed as the world&#x27;s highest, with a glass-shielded terrace over Victoria Harbour and the Island skyline. Cocktails with Asian twists, plus a champagne Sunday brunch.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The dramatic skyline-from-above bar; locally a special-occasion pick. Reserve a window for dusk (sunset about 17:46) and note the smart-casual dress code.</p><p>iconic · West Kowloon</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=OZONE%20%28Ritz-Carlton%20Hong%20Kong%29%2C%20118/F%2C%20The%20Ritz-Carlton%2C%20International%20Commerce%20Centre%2C%201%20Austin%20Road%20West%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.160226,22.303379,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kowloon Taproom</name><description><![CDATA[<p>TST bar built around Hong Kong craft beer on rotating taps; local pours have included Hong Kong Beer Co and Lion Rock Brewery.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Handy local-craft-beer stop on Ashley Road after the harbourfront. Tap lists rotate, so ask for the current local selection.</p><p>niche · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kowloon%20Taproom%2C%20G/F%2C%2017-19%20Ashley%20Road%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.170491,22.297347,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yardleys Taproom</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Soho taproom of local brewery Yardley Brothers, pouring its own beers on Peel Street.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A brewery-owned local craft room in Soho, near the Mid-Levels Escalator and the market end of Graham Street.</p><p>niche · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Yardleys%20Taproom%2C%20G/F%2C%2062%20Peel%20Street%2C%20Soho%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.152056,22.282014,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sun Hing Restaurant (Kennedy Town)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Dim sum house that opens at 03:00 and serves until 16:00; the custard &#x27;liu sha&#x27; bun, curry tripe, shrimp dumplings and crab-roe siu mai are the draws.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The classic overnight &#x27;night tea&#x27; stop for the post-club crowd and early shift workers, per HK01&#x27;s May 2026 supper roundup. An after-midnight meal on 26 or 27 Dec before sunrise at the Kennedy Town promenade.</p><p>niche · Kennedy Town</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sun%20Hing%20Restaurant%20%28Kennedy%20Town%29%2C%20Shop%20C%2C%20G/F%2C%208%20Sze%20Mei%20Road%2C%20Kennedy%20Town%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.128245,22.282951,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Miu Kai Siu Sik (Temple Street skewers and satay noodles)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Charcoal-grilled skewers (squid, pork neck, chicken kidney) and satay beef noodles, open 18:00 to 03:00, next to the Temple Street night market.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A lively after-dinner supper stop in Yau Ma Tei; named in HK01&#x27;s May 2026 supper guide, so useful after Temple Street or the fruit market.</p><p>niche · Yau Ma Tei</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Miu%20Kai%20Siu%20Sik%20%28Temple%20Street%20skewers%20and%20satay%20noodles%29%2C%20Shop%205%2C%20Wing%20Sing%20Lane%2C%20Yau%20Ma%20Tei%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.171409,22.310933,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Shopping &amp; Products</name>
<Placemark><name>Kowloon Soy Company</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Traditional soy sauce and condiment shop founded in 1917, now the remaining retail outlet of Kowloon Soy Co. Sells soy sauces, pickles and fermented tofu in the Graham Street market lane.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A century-old Hong Kong soy maker whose top-tier sauce is fermented in clay jars and sold locally; easy pantry souvenir, with plastic bottles available for flights.</p><p>strong · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kowloon%20Soy%20Company%2C%20G/F%2C%209%20Graham%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.154202,22.283758,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>G.O.D. (Goods of Desire), Hollywood Road</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Flagship-area store of Hong Kong lifestyle brand G.O.D., selling homewares, apparel and gifts with a nostalgic Hong Kong-slang humour.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hong Kong design label (founded by Douglas Young) that repackages local nostalgia: tong lau, mahjong, bing sutt themes.</p><p>strong · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=G.O.D.%20%28Goods%20of%20Desire%29%2C%20Hollywood%20Road%2C%2048%20Hollywood%20Road%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.153054,22.28242,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>PMQ (Police Married Quarters)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Former police married quarters turned creative hub with designer studios, shops, cafes and courtyard events.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The main place to meet Hong Kong independent designers and makers in one building; has run Christmas bazaars and displays in past years.</p><p>strong · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=PMQ%20%28Police%20Married%20Quarters%29%2C%2035%20Aberdeen%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.151791,22.283154,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ladies&#x27; Market (Tung Choi Street)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Long street of stalls selling clothes, accessories, souvenirs and phone cases; bargaining is the norm.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hong Kong&#x27;s best-known bargain street market, in the middle of the Mong Kok market cluster.</p><p>iconic · Mong Kok</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Ladies%27%20Market%20%28Tung%20Choi%20Street%29%2C%20Tung%20Choi%20Street%2C%20Mong%20Kok%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.171037,22.317051,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Temple Street Night Market</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Evening street market of stalls selling souvenirs, menswear, electronics and trinkets, with food stalls and fortune tellers nearby.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Classic Hong Kong night-market atmosphere; the street is a part-time pedestrian zone and popular with photographers after dark.</p><p>iconic · Yau Ma Tei</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Temple%20Street%20Night%20Market%2C%20Temple%20Street%2C%20between%20Jordan%20Road%20and%20Kansu%20Street%2C%20Yau%20Ma%20Tei%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.169965,22.306397,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Upper Lascar Row (Cat Street)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Pedestrian lane of small family-run stalls and shops selling ceramics, jade, Cultural Revolution memorabilia and vintage Hong Kong collectibles.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Cheaper, more casual cousin of Hollywood Road&#x27;s antique dealers; good for browsing even if buying nothing.</p><p>strong · Sheung Wan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Upper%20Lascar%20Row%20%28Cat%20Street%29%2C%20Upper%20Lascar%20Row%2C%20Sheung%20Wan%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.149658,22.284889,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sham Shui Po fabric, leather and paper streets</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Grid of wholesale-style shops for fabric, buttons, lace, leather and paper. The old Yen Chow Street fabric bazaar moved in 2023 to a market on Tung Chau Street.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hong Kong&#x27;s craft and haberdashery heartland; shops like Wa Fat Piece Goods (1J Shek Kip Mei St), Luen Hing Paper (168 Kiu Kiang St) and Brothers Leathercraft (207-209 Tai Nan St) serve designers and hobbyists.</p><p>strong · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sham%20Shui%20Po%20fabric%2C%20leather%20and%20paper%20streets%2C%20Ki%20Lung%2C%20Nam%20Cheong%2C%20Tai%20Nan%2C%20Kiu%20Kiang%20and%20Shek%20Kip%20Mei%20Streets%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.161621,22.329126,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Apliu Street flea market</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Street of hawker stalls and small shops selling electronics, phone parts, hi-fi, camera gear and odd secondhand items.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hong Kong&#x27;s electronics bazaar; Ying Kee Trading at 154 Apliu St sells photo accessories.</p><p>strong · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Apliu%20Street%20flea%20market%2C%20Apliu%20Street%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%20%28MTR%20exit%20A2/C2%29%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.166056,22.327067,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Good Bad Creative</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Secondhand shop full of 1980s-90s toys, cassettes and magazines.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Good for Hong Kong pop-culture nostalgia in Sham Shui Po&#x27;s indie-shop cluster.</p><p>niche · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Good%20Bad%20Creative%2C%20Shop%20B1%2C%20124%20Nam%20Cheong%20Street%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.163383,22.328951,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hak Dei</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Retro-minded shop stocking local ceramics, bamboo steamers and kitchen gadgets.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A convenient place to pick up Hong Kong-style bamboo steamers and kitchen pieces in one stop.</p><p>niche · Mong Kok</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hak%20Dei%2C%20G/F%2C%20618%20Shanghai%20Street%2C%20Mong%20Kok%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.168179,22.319585,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dongpohk</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Neighbourhood shop in a historic tenement selling local snacks, toys and vintage pieces.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Tong lau-set shop that mixes everyday Hong Kong goods with vintage finds.</p><p>niche · Sheung Wan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Dongpohk%2C%2017%20Shing%20Wong%20Street%2C%20Sheung%20Wan%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.150939,22.28319,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>JikSap</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Large vintage store in an industrial building selling prints, toys and furniture.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Pairs well with a 13 Streets walk in To Kwa Wan.</p><p>niche · To Kwa Wan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=JikSap%2C%209/F%2C%20Block%20A%2C%20On%20Lok%20Factory%20Building%2C%2090%20Kowloon%20City%20Road%2C%20To%20Kwa%20Wan%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.191257,22.322956,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Muze Pens</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Fine pens, inks and premium paper, with products available to try.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Sits comfortably in Sham Shui Po&#x27;s paper and craft area for stationery fans.</p><p>niche · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Muze%20Pens%2C%20G/F%2C%20236%20Tai%20Nan%20Street%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.165905,22.325144,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kobo Camera</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Small shop near the Ladies&#x27; Market selling new film cameras and a curated vintage selection, with staff happy to help beginners.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A friendly entry point to Hong Kong&#x27;s film camera scene.</p><p>niche · Mong Kok</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kobo%20Camera%2C%20Room%20706%2C%207/F%2C%20Witty%20Commercial%20Building%2C%201A-1L%20Tung%20Choi%20Street%2C%20Mong%20Kok%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.171128,22.316104,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sunrise Photo Co</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Photo shop established in 1973 offering printing and a large secondhand film camera and lens selection; also buys used cameras. Has a cat mascot.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Old-school Sham Shui Po camera dealer; combine with Apliu Street.</p><p>niche · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sunrise%20Photo%20Co%2C%20Shop%20B2%2C%20333%20Lai%20Chi%20Kok%20Road%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.159934,22.328723,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dried seafood street (Des Voeux Road West)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Trade street of baskets of dried abalone, scallops, sea cucumber, fish maw and dried mushrooms; Wing Lok Street leans to ginseng and bird&#x27;s nest.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Last visible stretch of Hong Kong&#x27;s historic dried-seafood trade; vivid and photogenic.</p><p>strong · Sheung Wan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Dried%20seafood%20street%20%28Des%20Voeux%20Road%20West%29%2C%20Des%20Voeux%20Road%20West%20between%20Queen%20Street%20and%20Main%20Street%2C%20plus%20Wing%20Lok%20Street%20and%20Bonham%20Strand%20West%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.14626,22.287067,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tuck Chong Sum Kee Bamboo Steamer Company</name><description><![CDATA[<p>One of the few Hong Kong shops still making bamboo steamers by hand, run by a fifth-generation owner and long a supplier to teahouses. Also sells bamboo household items.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A genuine last-of-their-trade craft shop; a handmade steamer is a distinctive Hong Kong kitchen souvenir.</p><p>strong · Sai Ying Pun</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tuck%20Chong%20Sum%20Kee%20Bamboo%20Steamer%20Company%2C%20G/F%2C%2012%20Western%20Street%2C%20Sai%20Ying%20Pun%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.140934,22.287471,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Goldfish Market (Tung Choi Street North)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Roughly 300 m stretch of shops selling goldfish, aquarium gear and other pets, hung with bags of fish.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Goldfish are a lucky symbol, and the street is a striking, only-in-Hong-Kong sight.</p><p>strong · Mong Kok</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Goldfish%20Market%20%28Tung%20Choi%20Street%20North%29%2C%20Tung%20Choi%20Street%20North%2C%20Mong%20Kok%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.168859,22.325778,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Flower Market Road</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Long avenue of florists and plant sellers spilling blooms onto the pavement.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Colourful and photogenic, and festive plants may be on display in late December.</p><p>strong · Prince Edward</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Flower%20Market%20Road%2C%20Flower%20Market%20Road%2C%20Prince%20Edward%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.172722,22.325194,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sneakers Street (Fa Yuen Street)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Street lined with sportswear and sneaker shops, from major brands to limited editions.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hong Kong&#x27;s best-known sneaker shopping strip; holiday sales are common.</p><p>strong · Mong Kok</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sneakers%20Street%20%28Fa%20Yuen%20Street%29%2C%20Fa%20Yuen%20Street%2C%20Mong%20Kok%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.16982,22.325699,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fook Ming Tong Tea Shop (IFC Mall)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Branch of the Hong Kong tea chain founded in 1987 that sells loose tea and teaware, with tasting. Also has outlets at Harbour City, a Causeway Bay SOGO counter and the airport.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A Hong Kong-founded tea brand with knowledgeable staff and tastings; easy tea souvenir.</p><p>niche · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Fook%20Ming%20Tong%20Tea%20Shop%20%28IFC%20Mall%29%2C%203/F%2C%20IFC%20Mall%2C%208%20Finance%20Street%2C%20Central%20%28unit%20listed%20as%203006%20or%203015%29%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.15892,22.284098,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>K11 MUSEA</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Art-led luxury mall on the Tsim Sha Tsui waterfront with direct access to the Avenue of Stars and Salisbury Garden.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The most design-driven mall in Kowloon; seasonal programmes and harbour-facing installations have appeared in past Decembers.</p><p>strong · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=K11%20MUSEA%2C%2018%20Salisbury%20Road%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.173837,22.293893,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Harbour City</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Hong Kong&#x27;s largest shopping complex, with a free open-air Ocean Terminal Forecourt Christmas display in recent years.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The 2025 season ran 12 Nov 2025 - 4 Jan 2026 with a Disney-themed 9 m centrepiece and evening snow effects, so a similar display is likely for Christmas 2026.</p><p>strong · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Harbour%20City%2C%20Canton%20Road%20and%20Ocean%20Terminal%20Forecourt%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.16842,22.297002,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kai Tak Mall (Kai Tak Sports Park)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Roughly 700,000 sq ft, three-building mall at the sports park (opened 2025) with around 200 stores, sports brands, a Liverpool FC flagship and about 80 restaurants.</p><p><b>Why:</b> New sports-led mall that drew a record New Year&#x27;s Eve crowd, and a gateway to the Kai Tak waterfront.</p><p>niche · Kai Tak</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kai%20Tak%20Mall%20%28Kai%20Tak%20Sports%20Park%29%2C%20Kai%20Tak%20Sports%20Park%2C%20Kai%20Tak%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.197812,22.323634,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Linva Tailor</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Traditional cheongsam tailor opened in 1965; custom garments are made by hand.</p><p><b>Why:</b> One of the last Shanghainese-style cheongsam makers, with few apprentices.</p><p>niche · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Linva%20Tailor%2C%2038%20Cochrane%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.154138,22.282685,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Choi Kee birdcage workshop (Yuen Po Street Bird Garden)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Stall of Chan Lok-choi, described as possibly Hong Kong&#x27;s last birdcage maker, who mostly repairs cages now. The garden itself is listed as open 07:00-20:00 daily.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A living last-of-trade craft inside a bird market that is good for photography.</p><p>niche · Prince Edward</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Choi%20Kee%20birdcage%20workshop%20%28Yuen%20Po%20Street%20Bird%20Garden%29%2C%20Yuen%20Po%20Street%20Bird%20Garden%2C%20Prince%20Edward%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.173813,22.325891,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Chinese Arts &amp; Crafts, Pacific Place</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Department-store-style shop for Chinese silks, porcelain, jade, embroidery and gifts.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Long-standing source of Chinese crafts, though reviews say the range has changed.</p><p>niche · Admiralty</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Chinese%20Arts%20%26%20Crafts%2C%20Pacific%20Place%2C%20Shop%20320%2C%20The%20Mall%2C%20Pacific%20Place%2C%2088%20Queensway%2C%20Admiralty%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.164467,22.277203,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Neighborhoods &amp; Walks</name>
<Placemark><name>Sham Shui Po</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Dense, lived-in district of fabric, button, leather and paper wholesalers, electronics stalls, old-school eateries and a growing set of indie shops.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The most authentic everyday-and-craft shopping neighbourhood in Kowloon, and good for street photography.</p><p>strong · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Around%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%20MTR%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.160851,22.329757,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>To Kwa Wan and the 13 Streets</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Grid of short parallel streets lined with late-1950s buildings, named after auspicious creatures like dragon, phoenix and crane, with small shops and cafes.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A quiet, textured old-Kowloon grid that few visitors see; pair with the nearby waterfront (Kowloon City Pier, Hoi Sham Park).</p><p>niche · To Kwa Wan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=To%20Kwa%20Wan%20and%20the%2013%20Streets%2C%20Between%20Kowloon%20City%20Road%2C%20To%20Kwa%20Wan%20Road%2C%20Mok%20Cheong%20Street%20and%20Ma%20Tau%20Kok%20Road%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.18954,22.316758,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kennedy Town</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Western end of the Island line with cafes along Forbes Street, a harbourfront and the Sai Wan Swimming Shed, the last such shed on the waterfront.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A calmer, still-local side of the Island; iDiscover offers a free self-guided memory trail.</p><p>niche · Kennedy Town</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kennedy%20Town%2C%20Forbes%20Street%2C%20Kennedy%20Town%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.12677,22.281232,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tai Hang</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Former village tucked behind Causeway Bay with indie cafes, small shops, Lin Fa Temple and the Tai Hang Fire Dragon Heritage Centre at 12 School Street.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Known for its Mid-Autumn Fire Dragon Dance and a cafe-and-shophouse stroll; the heritage centre shows the tradition year-round.</p><p>strong · Tai Hang</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tai%20Hang%2C%20Wun%20Sha%20Street%20and%20Lin%20Fa%20Temple%20area%2C%20Tai%20Hang%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.193151,22.277908,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sai Ying Pun</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Hillside grid of early-developed streets (First, Second, Third, High) with cafes, restaurants, Sai Ying Pun Market at 44-45 Centre Street and the Centre Street escalator link.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Old Hong Kong grid with newer cafes and wine bars; steep, photogenic and close to Sheung Wan.</p><p>strong · Sai Ying Pun</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sai%20Ying%20Pun%2C%20Second%20Street%20and%20Centre%20Street%2C%20Sai%20Ying%20Pun%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.142086,22.286121,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kwun Tong</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Former factory district whose industrial buildings now host studios, cafes and small restaurants, with a roughly 1 km waterfront promenade facing the Kai Tak cruise terminal.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A very different, utilitarian-creative Hong Kong; the promenade gives night views toward Hong Kong Island East.</p><p>niche · Kwun Tong</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kwun%20Tong%2C%20Hung%20To%20Road%20and%20Kwun%20Tong%20Promenade%2C%20Kwun%20Tong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.226512,22.312104,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Quarry Bay and Tai Koo</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Home of the Monster Building (an E-shaped 1960s housing complex of five connected blocks) and Taikoo Place&#x27;s Sunday market on Tong Chong Street.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Iconic dense-housing photography; shoot from the street only, since the inner courtyard is now closed to the public.</p><p>strong · Quarry Bay</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Quarry%20Bay%20and%20Tai%20Koo%2C%20King%27s%20Road%20near%20Yick%20Cheong%20Building%2C%20Quarry%20Bay%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.21292,22.285795,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yau Ma Tei</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Old Kowloon quarter with the Tin Hau Temple, Shanghai Street&#x27;s cookware and craft shops, the Jade Market and the 1913 wholesale fruit market (Grade II) on Reclamation Street.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Concentrated old-Kowloon life; the fruit market works overnight to morning, so visit respectfully.</p><p>strong · Yau Ma Tei</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Yau%20Ma%20Tei%2C%20Temple%20Street%2C%20Shanghai%20Street%20and%20Reclamation%20Street%2C%20Yau%20Ma%20Tei%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.170794,22.312373,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tai O</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Fishing village on west Lantau with stilt houses, a small dried-seafood market and shops selling shrimp paste, salted fish and dried shrimp. The Tai O Heritage Hotel is a converted 1902 police station.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The best place to buy the village&#x27;s signature shrimp paste and salted fish; Yik Cheong is a well-known shop and Ha Chu Beng on Kat Hing Street serves pancakes cooked with the local paste.</p><p>strong · Tai O</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tai%20O%2C%20Kat%20Hing%20Street%20and%20the%20creek%2C%20Tai%20O%2C%20Lantau%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>113.86362,22.254152,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sheung Wan</name><description><![CDATA[<p>One of the city&#x27;s oldest districts: dried-seafood shops on Wing Lok Street, antique and gallery shops along Hollywood Road, Man Mo Temple, steep Ladder Street, and street art and indie shops around Tai Ping Shan Street.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Old Chinese trades and new design side by side on foot; works as a walking spine from Wing Lok Street up to Man Mo Temple and Tai Ping Shan.</p><p>strong · Sheung Wan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sheung%20Wan%2C%20Wing%20Lok%20Street%20/%20Hollywood%20Road%2C%20Sheung%20Wan%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.150208,22.286516,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kowloon City</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A former walled-city and airport-neighbour district known as Hong Kong&#x27;s Little Thailand, with Thai restaurants and grocers, Kowloon Walled City Park, the Kowloon City market cooked food centre with dai pai dong upstairs, and old tong lau streets.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The best neighbourhood for a Thai-Chiu Chow food crawl and for old-Kai-Tak texture; reached by Sung Wong Toi or Kai Tak MTR.</p><p>strong · Kowloon City</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kowloon%20City%2C%20Nga%20Tsin%20Long%20Road%20/%20Kai%20Tak%20Road%20area%2C%20Kowloon%20City%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.189937,22.33016,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mong Kok</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Dense market district: Ladies&#x27; Market on Tung Choi Street, Goldfish Street, Fa Yuen Street sneaker shops, Flower Market Road and Yuen Po Street Bird Garden, with street snacks throughout.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The most concentrated street-market energy in the city, busy late; combine a morning bird-garden/flower-market walk with an evening market run.</p><p>strong · Mong Kok</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Mong%20Kok%2C%20Nathan%20Road%20/%20Tung%20Choi%20Street%2C%20Mong%20Kok%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.169808,22.319308,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Photography</name>
<Placemark><name>Choi Hung Estate basketball court</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Pastel-coloured 1960s public housing blocks around a rooftop basketball court.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hong Kong&#x27;s most photographed housing estate and still a working home.</p><p>iconic · Choi Hung</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Choi%20Hung%20Estate%20basketball%20court%2C%202%20Tse%20Wai%20Avenue%2C%20Ngau%20Chi%20Wan%20%28rooftop%20car%20park%29%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.207054,22.333917,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ping Shek Estate</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Curved concrete blocks with a central courtyard for upward symmetry shots.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Quieter than Choi Hung, five minutes away.</p><p>niche · Choi Hung</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Ping%20Shek%20Estate%2C%20Near%20Choi%20Hung%20MTR%20Exit%20A2%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.209126,22.332922,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Monster Building (Montane Mansion, Yick Cheong and Yick Fat)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Interlinked 1980s residential blocks forming a dense facade.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hollywood-famous HK density image.</p><p>iconic · Quarry Bay</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Monster%20Building%20%28Montane%20Mansion%2C%20Yick%20Cheong%20and%20Yick%20Fat%29%2C%20King%27s%20Road%2C%20Quarry%20Bay%20%28Taikoo%20MTR%29%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.211966,22.284104,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mong Kok and Yau Ma Tei neon</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The remaining neon signs, such as the Gam Lai Gung Karaoke signs and Wo Hing Victory Mahjong at 385 Nathan Road.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Signs keep coming down under building codes; a few were removed in 2026.</p><p>strong · Mong Kok</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Mong%20Kok%20and%20Yau%20Ma%20Tei%20neon%2C%20Nathan%20Road%2C%20Mong%20Kok%20and%20Yau%20Ma%20Tei%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.169808,22.319308,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lugard Road lookout</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A free skyline vantage on the Peak Circle Walk.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Same view as Sky Terrace 428 without the fee or the crowds.</p><p>strong · The Peak</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lugard%20Road%20lookout%2C%20Lugard%20Road%2C%20The%20Peak%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.14809,22.277092,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Instagram Pier (Western District)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Working cargo pier popular for photos.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Marine Department warns of fines for trespassing.</p><p>niche · Sai Ying Pun</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Instagram%20Pier%20%28Western%20District%29%2C%20Western%20District%20Public%20Cargo%20Working%20Area%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.133309,22.288595,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kennedy Town Promenade and harbour views</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Steep streets drop straight to the harbour; a nearby playground basketball court frames Catchick Street and the water through a fence.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A sunset vantage with fewer visitors than Central.</p><p>niche · Kennedy Town</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kennedy%20Town%20Promenade%20and%20harbour%20views%2C%20New%20Praya%2C%20Kennedy%20Town%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.129019,22.284163,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tsim Sha Tsui Promenade and Avenue of Stars</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The harbourfront promenade with a head-on view of the Central skyline and the nightly Symphony of Lights at 20:00.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The classic skyline shot; less crowded at sunrise.</p><p>iconic · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%20Promenade%20and%20Avenue%20of%20Stars%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%20waterfront%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.185648,22.299419,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Braemar Hill lookout</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A hillside lookout reached by a 20-30 minute unmarked walk, recommended for harbour sunset.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A local-photographer favourite; haze in winter can hide towers.</p><p>niche · North Point</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Braemar%20Hill%20lookout%2C%20Braemar%20Hill%2C%20North%20Point%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.198849,22.283441,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kowloon Peak (Fei Ngo Shan)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Late-afternoon viewpoint over Kowloon estates and mountain ranges.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hard hike, big reward.</p><p>niche · Choi Hung</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kowloon%20Peak%20%28Fei%20Ngo%20Shan%29%2C%20Fei%20Ngo%20Shan%20Road%2C%20Ma%20On%20Shan%20Country%20Park%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.228927,22.336424,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Graham Street mural and market</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A bright mural beside a long-running street market.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Narrow street; traffic safety issues cited.</p><p>niche · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Graham%20Street%20mural%20and%20market%2C%20Graham%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.154045,22.283511,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ocean Terminal Deck</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Waterfront deck where junk boats drift past at sunset.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Sunset spot with a dependable harbour view.</p><p>niche · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Ocean%20Terminal%20Deck%2C%20Harbour%20City%2C%20Canton%20Road%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.16842,22.297002,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>West Kowloon Waterfront Promenade</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Open promenade with harbour views, beside M+ and the Palace Museum.</p><p><b>Why:</b> An easy skyline shot any hour.</p><p>strong · West Kowloon</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=West%20Kowloon%20Waterfront%20Promenade%2C%20West%20Kowloon%20Cultural%20District%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.162984,22.301709,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lee Tung Avenue</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Pedestrian street of shops and restaurants on the old Wedding Card Street, which has staged Christmas light and snow displays in past years.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A walkable festive stop with heritage tie-ins to Wan Chai&#x27;s wedding-card printers.</p><p>niche · Wan Chai</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lee%20Tung%20Avenue%2C%20Lee%20Tung%20Avenue%2C%20Wan%20Chai%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.172419,22.275775,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yau Ma Tei Wholesale Fruit Market</name><description><![CDATA[<p>1913 wholesale fruit market (Grade III historic) whose workers begin around midnight, stacking crates and loading carts under old shopfronts.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Time Out cites it among the best street-photography spots; the lit crates and pre-dawn labour suit film and candid work.</p><p>niche · Yau Ma Tei</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Yau%20Ma%20Tei%20Wholesale%20Fruit%20Market%2C%20Shek%20Lung%20Street%2C%20Yau%20Ma%20Tei%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.168449,22.311982,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Central-Mid-Levels Escalator</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The world&#x27;s longest covered outdoor escalator system, threading past Soho, Hollywood Road and tower blocks; the Queen&#x27;s Road to Hollywood Road stretch is the photographic core.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Layers of tong lau, shops and commuters from above; near the Graham Street mural and Man Mo Temple. Free.</p><p>strong · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Central-Mid-Levels%20Escalator%2C%20Cochrane%20Street%20to%20Conduit%20Road%2C%20Central-Mid-Levels%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.150745,22.279105,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>sky100 Observation Deck (ICC)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Indoor observation deck on the 100th floor of ICC with 360-degree harbour views.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Highest public viewing deck, but it closed for renovation from 17 May 2025 with a reopening target of H1 2026 that I could not confirm. Free alternatives: TST promenade and West Kowloon waterfront.</p><p>niche · West Kowloon</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=sky100%20Observation%20Deck%20%28ICC%29%2C%20100/F%2C%20International%20Commerce%20Centre%2C%201%20Austin%20Road%20West%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.160226,22.303379,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Landmarks &amp; Culture</name>
<Placemark><name>Tai Kwun Centre for Heritage and Arts</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Heritage centre in the former Central Police Station with several independent shops: TASCHEN (books, 11:00-19:00), LockCha Tea Shop (tea and Yixing teaware, 11:00-19:00), Bonart (botanical art), and Yuen&#x27;s Tailor (bespoke, founded 1977).</p><p><b>Why:</b> The most complete colonial-era law-and-order compound in the city, free to wander, with Hollywood Road and SoHo restaurants at its doorstep. Good for architecture and history fans and anyone wanting a rainy-day or evening anchor.</p><p>iconic · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tai%20Kwun%20Centre%20for%20Heritage%20and%20Arts%2C%2010%20Hollywood%20Road%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.154009,22.281156,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hong Kong Palace Museum</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Museum of Chinese imperial art with loans from Beijing&#x27;s Palace Museum, in a building by Rocco Design Architects inspired by traditional Chinese forms, set on the West Kowloon harbourfront.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Rare chance to see Forbidden City treasures in Hong Kong; combines culture with the WKCD waterfront.</p><p>iconic · West Kowloon</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hong%20Kong%20Palace%20Museum%2C%208%20Museum%20Drive%2C%20West%20Kowloon%20Cultural%20District%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.155398,22.301649,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>M+</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Herzog &amp; de Meuron-designed museum of 20th and 21st century visual culture covering design, architecture, moving image and art, with a LED-facade towards the harbour.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Asia&#x27;s flagship visual-culture museum; the building, harbour skyline views from the terrace and the Friday late opening make it a design pick. Current special exhibition is Design Ah! (to 10 Jan 2027).</p><p>iconic · West Kowloon</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=M%2B%2C%2038%20Museum%20Drive%2C%20West%20Kowloon%20Cultural%20District%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.159147,22.301054,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hong Kong Museum of Art</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The government&#x27;s art museum on the TST waterfront, reopened in 2019, with Chinese painting and calligraphy, Cantonese export art and local art shows.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Cheap, central and open until 21:00 on holidays, so a good Christmas evening culture stop; current shows include a Cantonese export art collection and Hong Kong at the Venice Biennale.</p><p>strong · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hong%20Kong%20Museum%20of%20Art%2C%2010%20Salisbury%20Road%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.171869,22.294169,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mei Ho House (Museum of Public Housing)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A surviving 1954 H-shaped Mark I resettlement block, restored as a youth hostel with a free heritage museum on early public housing life.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Tells the story of the 1953 Shek Kip Mei fire and how HK public housing began; ideal for retro and social-history fans, and pairs with Sham Shui Po food and fabric streets.</p><p>niche · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Mei%20Ho%20House%20%28Museum%20of%20Public%20Housing%29%2C%20Block%2041%2C%20Shek%20Kip%20Mei%20Estate%2C%20Berwick%20Street%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.163692,22.3339,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Chi Lin Nunnery and Nan Lian Garden</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A Tang-dynasty-style timber Buddhist nunnery beside a classical Chinese garden with ponds, rockeries and a red wooden bridge, both free to enter.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The best place in the city for traditional Chinese architecture and calm; ideal for architecture and photography fans, and close to Wong Tai Sin.</p><p>iconic · Diamond Hill</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Chi%20Lin%20Nunnery%20and%20Nan%20Lian%20Garden%2C%205%20Chi%20Lin%20Drive%2C%20Diamond%20Hill%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.205639,22.340064,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wong Tai Sin Temple</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Hong Kong&#x27;s most popular Taoist temple, with bright red-and-gold halls, a fortune-telling arcade and gardens, dedicated to the healing deity Wong Tai Sin.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Living religious practice (kau cim fortune sticks) on a grand scale; for culture lovers and fortune-seekers, with Chi Lin nearby.</p><p>iconic · Wong Tai Sin</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Wong%20Tai%20Sin%20Temple%2C%202%20Chuk%20Yuen%20Village%2C%20Wong%20Tai%20Sin%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.193551,22.342818,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Man Mo Temple</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A 19th-century Taoist temple dedicated to the gods of literature and war, filled with huge hanging incense coils.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Atmospheric incense-smoke interiors in the middle of Hollywood Road antique shops; best for photographers and history fans.</p><p>strong · Sheung Wan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Man%20Mo%20Temple%2C%20124-126%20Hollywood%20Road%2C%20Sheung%20Wan%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.150158,22.283871,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ten Thousand Buddhas Monastery</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A hillside monastery reached by about 400 steps lined with golden Buddha statues, with a hall holding thousands of small Buddhas.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Quirky and uncommercial; good for those who like unusual religious art.</p><p>niche · Sha Tin</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Ten%20Thousand%20Buddhas%20Monastery%2C%20Pai%20Tau%20Village%2C%20Sha%20Tin%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.187152,22.384476,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Po Lin Monastery and Tian Tan Buddha</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A monastery with the 34-metre bronze Big Buddha on a hilltop, reached by the Ngong Ping 360 cable car or bus.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hong Kong&#x27;s best-known outdoor religious landmark; the cable car adds scenic views.</p><p>iconic · Ngong Ping</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Po%20Lin%20Monastery%20and%20Tian%20Tan%20Buddha%2C%20Ngong%20Ping%2C%20Lantau%20Island%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>113.90416,22.255803,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Viva Blue House and Hong Kong House of Stories</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A 1920s-50s tong lau cluster (Blue, Yellow, Orange houses) still home to residents, with a small museum and free guided tours.</p><p><b>Why:</b> UNESCO-awarded conservation of a living tenement; ideal for retro and architecture fans.</p><p>niche · Wan Chai</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Viva%20Blue%20House%20and%20Hong%20Kong%20House%20of%20Stories%2C%2072A%20Stone%20Nullah%20Lane%2C%20Wan%20Chai%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.173931,22.273767,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lui Seng Chun</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A 1931 corner tong lau with a mix of Chinese and Western design, now an HKBU Chinese medicine clinic and heritage centre.</p><p><b>Why:</b> One of the best-preserved tong lau in Kowloon.</p><p>niche · Mong Kok</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lui%20Seng%20Chun%2C%20119%20Lai%20Chi%20Kok%20Road%2C%20Mong%20Kok%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.165139,22.324749,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Former North Kowloon Magistracy (NK Future)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A 1960 Grade 2 historic building, former art college site, being turned into a judicial education centre called NK Future.</p><p><b>Why:</b> SCAD Hong Kong no longer operates there, as its lease ended in July 2020.</p><p>niche · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Former%20North%20Kowloon%20Magistracy%20%28NK%20Future%29%2C%20Tonkin%20Street%20West%2C%20Sham%20Shui%20Po%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.153501,22.329923,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hong Kong Museum of History</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Permanent Hong Kong Story exhibition reopened 1 April 2026 after a renovation begun in 2020, now on the ground floor only.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The main local-history museum; media dispute the revised content.</p><p>strong · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hong%20Kong%20Museum%20of%20History%2C%20100%20Chatham%20Road%20South%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%20East%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.177251,22.301935,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lei Cheng Uk Han Tomb Museum</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A brick tomb from the Eastern Han period preserved in a small museum.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hong Kong&#x27;s oldest dated structure; free.</p><p>niche · Sham Shui Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lei%20Cheng%20Uk%20Han%20Tomb%20Museum%2C%2041%20Tonkin%20Street%2C%20Cheung%20Sha%20Wan%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.16001,22.33801,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hong Kong Maritime Museum</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Museum on the Central waterfront.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Open 10:00-19:00 on Sat, Sun and public holidays, closed only on CNY days 1-2.</p><p>niche · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hong%20Kong%20Maritime%20Museum%2C%20Central%20Pier%208%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.158278,22.281829,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kai Tak Sports Park</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Populous-designed 28-hectare complex with a retractable-roof stadium, opened March 2025.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Modern landmark; event days make crowds.</p><p>strong · Kai Tak</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kai%20Tak%20Sports%20Park%2C%20Kai%20Tak%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.198949,22.330105,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Peak Tram and Peak Tower</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The 1888 funicular up to Victoria Peak, with the Sky Terrace 428 viewing deck at the top.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The classic harbour view; queues are long on holidays, so go early or at dusk.</p><p>iconic · The Peak</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Peak%20Tram%20and%20Peak%20Tower%2C%20Lower%20Terminus%2C%2033%20Garden%20Road%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.159176,22.27767,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Experiences &amp; Events</name>
<Placemark><name>Sha Tin Racing, Sat 26 Dec (Boxing Day meeting)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A day race meeting at Sha Tin Racecourse on the Saturday after Christmas, per a third-party compilation of the HKJC 2026/27 calendar. There is no meeting on 25 or 27 Dec; the nearest Happy Valley night meeting is Wed 23 Dec.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Festive-season racing is a long-standing Hong Kong tradition and the most accessible way to see local betting culture; tourists can enter the public stands cheaply.</p><p>strong · Sha Tin</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sha%20Tin%20Racing%2C%20Sat%2026%20Dec%20%28Boxing%20Day%20meeting%29%2C%20Sha%20Tin%20Racecourse%2C%20Sha%20Tin%2C%20New%20Territories%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.204363,22.399726,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>WinterFest 2026-27: Christmas Town and Central light show (dates TBC)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>HKTB&#x27;s annual winter festival; the last edition centred on a 20 m tree, a free Christmas Town with a chalet market at Statue Square Gardens, and a building-light show across eight Central landmarks.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The city&#x27;s signature Christmas photo-op and market, free and walkable from Central/Admiralty.</p><p>iconic · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=WinterFest%202026-27%3A%20Christmas%20Town%20and%20Central%20light%20show%20%28dates%20TBC%29%2C%20Statue%20Square%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.160117,22.281775,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hong Kong Ballet: The Nutcracker (A Hong Kong Adventure)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Septime Webre&#x27;s Nutcracker re-set in early 20th-century Hong Kong, danced by Hong Kong Ballet at the Cultural Centre Grand Theatre; 2025 was its fifth year.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A uniquely Hong Kong take on the Christmas classic, with Star Ferry and local-landmark scenography.</p><p>strong · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hong%20Kong%20Ballet%3A%20The%20Nutcracker%20%28A%20Hong%20Kong%20Adventure%29%2C%20Grand%20Theatre%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20Cultural%20Centre%2C%2010%20Salisbury%20Road%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.170459,22.293722,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>M+: Myths and Monsters: The Art of Fantasy in Asia</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Major M+ exhibition on fantasy imagery across Asian art, film, games and manga, in the West Gallery.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A headline show at West Kowloon&#x27;s contemporary museum, running through the whole trip.</p><p>strong · West Kowloon</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=M%2B%3A%20Myths%20and%20Monsters%3A%20The%20Art%20of%20Fantasy%20in%20Asia%2C%20M%2B%2C%2038%20Museum%20Drive%2C%20West%20Kowloon%20Cultural%20District%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.159147,22.301054,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>M+: Design Ah! Experience the Wonder of Everyday Design</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Interactive family-friendly design exhibition in the M+ Main Hall Gallery.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hands-on, good for a holiday afternoon with kids or design fans.</p><p>niche · West Kowloon</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=M%2B%3A%20Design%20Ah%21%20Experience%20the%20Wonder%20of%20Everyday%20Design%2C%20M%2B%2C%2038%20Museum%20Drive%2C%20West%20Kowloon%20Cultural%20District%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.159147,22.301054,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>M+: Lin Tianmiao and Wang Gongxin: Here and There</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Two-artist show of Chinese contemporary video and installation art; opens 4 Dec 2026.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Fresh opening just before the trip; pairs with Herzog &amp; de Meuron: In Focus (to be ongoing) and the free Xu Bing installation on the Grand Stair (to 3 Jan 2027).</p><p>niche · West Kowloon</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=M%2B%3A%20Lin%20Tianmiao%20and%20Wang%20Gongxin%3A%20Here%20and%20There%2C%20M%2B%2C%2038%20Museum%20Drive%2C%20West%20Kowloon%20Cultural%20District%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.159147,22.301054,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hong Kong Disneyland: A Disney Christmas (2026 dates TBC)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Seasonal Christmas programme with decorations, tree lighting, shows and snow effects.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Large, reliably annual festive attraction; a Chinese-language report mentions 20 Nov 2026 to 3 Jan 2027 with a new winter market, but that is not confirmed officially.</p><p>niche · Tung Chung</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hong%20Kong%20Disneyland%3A%20A%20Disney%20Christmas%20%282026%20dates%20TBC%29%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20Disneyland%20Resort%2C%20Lantau%20Island%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.040985,22.31307,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>A Symphony of Lights: suspended and being replaced</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Victoria Harbour&#x27;s nightly 8 pm light and laser show, running since 2004. The 2026-27 budget announced it would end in 2H 2026 and be replaced by light installations at various places and times rather than a nightly show.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Don&#x27;t plan around the nightly show: the government suspended it on 9 Sep 2026 until further notice; a farewell event was being considered and the Peak was floated as a site for the new installations.</p><p>strong · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=A%20Symphony%20of%20Lights%3A%20suspended%20and%20being%20replaced%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%20Promenade%2C%20Salisbury%20Road%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.185648,22.299419,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tai Kwun Christmas programme (2026 TBC)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Tai Kwun&#x27;s heritage compound hosted a 12 m tree, nightly light show, music and a circus show on the Parade Ground last Christmas.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A free, atmospheric Central heritage setting for Christmas photos.</p><p>niche · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tai%20Kwun%20Christmas%20programme%20%282026%20TBC%29%2C%20Tai%20Kwun%2C%2010%20Hollywood%20Road%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.154009,22.281156,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Star Ferry (Central to Tsim Sha Tsui)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The green-and-white harbour ferries that cross Victoria Harbour in about 9 minutes, running roughly 06:30-23:30.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The cheapest harbour cruise in the world and a cornerstone of old Hong Kong; the upper deck at dusk gives skyline shots from the water.</p><p>iconic · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Star%20Ferry%20%28Central%20to%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%29%2C%20Central%20Pier%207%20/%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%20Pier%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.160359,22.286628,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ngong Ping 360 cable car (Crystal Cabin)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Lantau cable car from Tung Chung to Ngong Ping with optional glass-floor Crystal Cabins, near Big Buddha.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Spectacular sea and mountain views, combined with Po Lin Monastery and Tai O.</p><p>strong · Ngong Ping</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Ngong%20Ping%20360%20cable%20car%20%28Crystal%20Cabin%29%2C%20Ngong%20Ping%20360%20Tung%20Chung%20Terminal%2C%2011%20Mei%20Tung%20Street%2C%20Tung%20Chung%2C%20Lantau%20Island%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>113.939429,22.28944,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tai O sampan ride and pink dolphin watching</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Short sampan rides through Tai O&#x27;s stilt-house channels, or dedicated Hong Kong DolphinWatch trips for Indo-Pacific humpback dolphins.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Fishing-village stilt houses and a chance at Hong Kong&#x27;s wild pink dolphins.</p><p>strong · Tai O</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tai%20O%20sampan%20ride%20and%20pink%20dolphin%20watching%2C%20Tai%20O%2C%20Lantau%20Island%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>113.86362,22.254152,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Neon sign workshop (Amazing Neon HK)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A 1.5-hour class bending a single-colour neon sign, from a studio in San Po Kong.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Neon is vanishing in HK; make your own piece.</p><p>niche · San Po Kong</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Neon%20sign%20workshop%20%28Amazing%20Neon%20HK%29%2C%20Flat%2013%2C%2018/F%2C%20Laurels%20Industrial%20Centre%2C%2032%20Tai%20Yau%20Street%2C%20San%20Po%20Kong%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.197475,22.337058,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Glocal Mahjong tile colouring workshop (PMQ)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A 1.5-hour guided workshop colouring engraved mahjong tiles at PMQ, for 2-4 guests.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hands-on mahjong craft at the PMQ design hub.</p><p>niche · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Glocal%20Mahjong%20tile%20colouring%20workshop%20%28PMQ%29%2C%20PMQ%2C%2035%20Aberdeen%20Street%2C%20Central%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.151846,22.283274,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hong Kong-style egg tart and mooncake class (Bake Your Own)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Two-hour private baking class making egg tarts and custard mooncakes, at Mira Place 1, TST.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Learn a Hong Kong staple.</p><p>niche · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hong%20Kong-style%20egg%20tart%20and%20mooncake%20class%20%28Bake%20Your%20Own%29%2C%20Shop%20407%2C%204/F%2C%20Mira%20Place%201%2C%20132%20Nathan%20Road%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.172232,22.296043,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Xiqu Centre Cantonese opera</name><description><![CDATA[<p>West Kowloon&#x27;s Chinese opera theatre with the Grand Theatre and the Tea House Theatre showcasing daily excerpts.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Best place to see Cantonese opera.</p><p>strong · West Kowloon</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Xiqu%20Centre%20Cantonese%20opera%2C%20Xiqu%20Centre%2C%2088%20Austin%20Road%20West%2C%20West%20Kowloon%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.167208,22.302069,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ding Ding tram and TramOramic open-top tour</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The 1904 double-deck tram line along the north shore of Hong Kong Island, plus a one-hour open-top sightseeing tour from Western Market or Causeway Bay.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A living heritage vehicle; the front upper-deck seat gives street-level views of tong lau density. Fare rose to HK$3.30 in 2026.</p><p>iconic · Central</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Ding%20Ding%20tram%20and%20TramOramic%20open-top%20tour%2C%20Tram%20line%20along%20Des%20Voeux%20Road%20/%20Hennessy%20Road%20/%20King%27s%20Road%2C%20Kennedy%20Town%20to%20Shau%20Kei%20Wan%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.150365,22.287309,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wong Tai Sin fortune-telling (kau chim)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Shake a canister of numbered sticks (kau chim) until one falls, then have a fortune-teller in the temple&#x27;s arcade interpret it; some speak English.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Rooted Cantonese folk practice at the city&#x27;s busiest temple; free to try yourself with the temple&#x27;s stick lookup, readings cost extra (reported from about HK$100).</p><p>strong · Wong Tai Sin</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Wong%20Tai%20Sin%20fortune-telling%20%28kau%20chim%29%2C%20Wong%20Tai%20Sin%20Temple%2C%202%20Chuk%20Yuen%20Village%2C%20Wong%20Tai%20Sin%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.193551,22.342818,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Morning tai chi at Victoria Park</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Informal early-morning tai chi groups practise in the park; visitors are often welcome to watch or join. The Hong Kong Tourism Board has also run free beginner classes (dates not found).</p><p><b>Why:</b> Authentic local ritual at no cost, easy before a Causeway Bay breakfast. Sunrise is about 07:00.</p><p>niche · Causeway Bay</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Morning%20tai%20chi%20at%20Victoria%20Park%2C%20Victoria%20Park%2C%20Causeway%20Road%2C%20Causeway%20Bay%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.189594,22.280864,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tai Pan Reflexology Parlour</name><description><![CDATA[<p>1940s-styled parlour doing foot reflexology with a leg soak in vintage barber-style chairs; a 2026 guide lists HK$400-700.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A themed take on the Cantonese foot-massage habit near Jordan; not a gritty local shop, so ask Chinese-language sources for neighbourhood parlours.</p><p>niche · Tsim Sha Tsui</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tai%20Pan%20Reflexology%20Parlour%2C%20B/F%2C%2083%20Nathan%20Road%2C%20Tsim%20Sha%20Tsui%2C%20Kowloon%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.172144,22.297324,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Nature &amp; Outdoors</name>
<Placemark><name>Dragon&#x27;s Back (Shek O Country Park)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A well-signposted ridge walk of roughly 7.5 km with sea views over Tai Long Wan, Shek O and Stanley, ending at Big Wave Bay or Shek O village.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The classic easy-to-reach HK Island hike: MTR to beach in a half day, with a swim-free coast ending at Shek O&#x27;s cafes. Best for first-time hikers and photographers wanting ridge-line sea views.</p><p>iconic · Shek O</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Dragon%27s%20Back%20%28Shek%20O%20Country%20Park%29%2C%20Trailhead%20at%20To%20Tei%20Wan%20on%20Shek%20O%20Road%2C%20Shek%20O%20Country%20Park%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.241993,22.239258,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lion Rock and Amah Rock (Lion Rock Country Park)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The peak that looms over Kowloon and gives Hong Kongers their &#x27;Lion Rock spirit&#x27;. Common routes are about 5 km from Lok Fu to Wong Tai Sin, with a short 15-minute detour to Amah Rock from the Hung Mui Kuk barbecue area.</p><p><b>Why:</b> The cultural symbol of the city, with a view straight down on the estates and across the harbour. Rewarding for photography at golden hour but the summit route has a steep exposed scramble.</p><p>iconic · Wong Tai Sin</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lion%20Rock%20and%20Amah%20Rock%20%28Lion%20Rock%20Country%20Park%29%2C%20Lion%20Rock%20Country%20Park%2C%20Kowloon%20%28common%20starts%3A%20Wong%20Tai%20Sin%20or%20Lok%20Fu%2C%20Amah%20Rock%20via%20Hung%20Mui%20Kuk%29%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.187166,22.353006,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lantau Peak via Lantau Trail Section 3</name><description><![CDATA[<p>The official 4.5 km, about 2.5 h section climbs Hong Kong&#x27;s second-highest peak (about 934 m) by a steep staircase, ending at Ngong Ping near the Big Buddha.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Sunrise hike of choice and a finish at the Big Buddha and Po Lin Monastery; it combines mountain and temple in one trip. For fit hikers; the steep ladder section is the crux.</p><p>strong · Ngong Ping</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lantau%20Peak%20via%20Lantau%20Trail%20Section%203%2C%20Pak%20Kung%20Au%20to%20Ngong%20Ping%2C%20Lantau%20Island%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>113.919939,22.249297,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sunset Peak (Lantau Trail Section 2)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Lantau Trail Section 2: about 6.5 km and 3.5 h with the climb to Sunset Peak (Tai Tung Shan), Hong Kong&#x27;s third highest peak, with wide views of the island and out to sea.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Named for its sunsets; a good alternative to the busier Lantau Peak, with grassy open ridges. Hikers in the know stay for the city lights, so bring a torch.</p><p>niche · Mui Wo</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sunset%20Peak%20%28Lantau%20Trail%20Section%202%29%2C%20Nam%20Shan%20to%20Pak%20Kung%20Au%2C%20Lantau%20Island%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>113.952727,22.257326,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sai Wan and Tai Long Wan beaches (MacLehose Trail Section 2)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Remote white-sand beaches in Sai Kung East Country Park reached on foot. Easiest route is from Sai Wan Pavilion by about an hour&#x27;s walk; longer routes start from the East Dam (about 6.6 km, 2 to 2.5 h to Sai Wan).</p><p><b>Why:</b> Often called the best beaches in Hong Kong; a classic winter day hike with sea views that feels far from the city. Strong rip currents and no lifeguards, so look not swim.</p><p>iconic · Sai Kung</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Sai%20Wan%20and%20Tai%20Long%20Wan%20beaches%20%28MacLehose%20Trail%20Section%202%29%2C%20Sai%20Wan%20Pavilion%20terminus%20%28Sai%20Kung%20East%20Country%20Park%29%3B%20also%20via%20High%20Island%20Reservoir%20East%20Dam%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.372845,22.399086,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>High Island Geo Trail (East Dam)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Easy flat walk beside the East Dam where hexagonal volcanic rock columns (about 140 million years old) and tetrapod breakwaters line the shore, part of the Hong Kong UNESCO Global Geopark.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hong Kong&#x27;s most dramatic geology with minimal effort; the columns are at Po Pin Chau by boat or the East Dam walk. Travellers who like geology and photography.</p><p>strong · Sai Kung</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=High%20Island%20Geo%20Trail%20%28East%20Dam%29%2C%20High%20Island%20Reservoir%20East%20Dam%2C%20Sai%20Kung%20East%20Country%20Park%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.371535,22.361976,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pat Sin Leng Nature Trail</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Official nature trail of 4 km and 2 h, rated moderate, with steep stone steps on a ridge of eight peaks overlooking Plover Cove and Starling Inlet.</p><p><b>Why:</b> One of Hong Kong&#x27;s most scenic ridgelines and less tourist-heavy; top choice for northern NT views in clear winter weather. Pairs with Plover Cove and Bride&#x27;s Pool.</p><p>strong · Tai Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Pat%20Sin%20Leng%20Nature%20Trail%2C%20Pat%20Sin%20Leng%20Country%20Park%2C%20Tai%20Po%20/%20Fanling%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.227941,22.486174,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Plover Cove Reservoir and Tai Mei Tuk</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A flat reservoir dam with water views, a cycle track along Tolo Harbour and the Bride&#x27;s Pool waterfall nearby, forming an easy day out from Tai Po.</p><p><b>Why:</b> A relaxed non-summit option; rent a bike at Tai Mei Tuk or walk the dam. Good for families and anyone avoiding steep climbs.</p><p>niche · Tai Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Plover%20Cove%20Reservoir%20and%20Tai%20Mei%20Tuk%2C%20Tai%20Mei%20Tuk%2C%20Plover%20Cove%20Country%20Park%2C%20Tai%20Po%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.260941,22.477411,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Peak Circle Walk (Lugard Road and Harlech Road)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>A mostly flat loop of about 3.5 km (1-1.5 h) with Lugard Road&#x27;s cliffside views over the harbour and Harlech Road shaded by trees and a small waterfall.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Free alternative to paying for the Sky Terrace, with the best harbour views and an easy walk. Suitable for anyone with children or limited fitness.</p><p>iconic · The Peak</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Peak%20Circle%20Walk%20%28Lugard%20Road%20and%20Harlech%20Road%29%2C%20Lugard%20Road%2C%20The%20Peak%20%28starts%20near%20Peak%20Tower%29%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.149195,22.271228,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tai Mo Shan (957 m)</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Hong Kong&#x27;s highest peak; the popular route runs from the visitor centre up Tai Mo Shan Road to the weather station.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Winter sea-of-cloud views; easier route is relatively gentle.</p><p>strong · Tsuen Wan</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tai%20Mo%20Shan%20%28957%20m%29%2C%20Tai%20Mo%20Shan%20Visitor%20Centre%2C%20Route%20Twisk%2C%20Tsuen%20Kam%20Au%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.124595,22.410117,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hong Kong Wetland Park</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Visitor centre and wetland reserve.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Winter bird season; open on public holidays.</p><p>niche · Yuen Long</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hong%20Kong%20Wetland%20Park%2C%20Wetland%20Park%20Road%2C%20Tin%20Shui%20Wai%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.005138,22.466446,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tung Ping Chau</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Remote Geopark island reached by ferry from Ma Liu Shui; weekend and public-holiday service only.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Ferry runs only weekends and holidays; Sunday 09:00 out, 17:15 back (Transport Department listing, undated).</p><p>strong · Tai Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tung%20Ping%20Chau%2C%20Ma%20Liu%20Shui%20Ferry%20Pier%2C%20Science%20Park%20Road%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.214413,22.417182,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>Day Trips</name>
<Placemark><name>Macau day trip</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Ferry (TurboJET to Outer Harbour, Cotai Water Jet to Taipa) from Sheung Wan about 50-55 min, or HZMB bus; fares about HK$175-200 by ferry.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Portuguese-Chinese old town, egg tarts, Senado Square, casinos.</p><p>iconic · Macau</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Macau%20day%20trip%2C%20Senado%20Square%2C%20Macau%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>113.53999,22.193827,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shenzhen day trip</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Cross at Lo Wu/Luohu or Futian by MTR East Rail then immigration.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Mainland markets and food.</p><p>niche · Shenzhen</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Shenzhen%20day%20trip%2C%20Futian%2C%20Shenzhen%2C%20Guangdong%2C%20China%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.049894,22.524363,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yim Tin Tsai</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Island with salt fields and St Joseph&#x27;s Chapel reached by kaito from Sai Kung pier.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Hakka and Catholic heritage.</p><p>niche · Sai Kung</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Yim%20Tin%20Tsai%2C%20Yim%20Tin%20Tsai%2C%20Sai%20Kung%2C%20New%20Territories%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.303746,22.377138,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lai Chi Wo</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Hakka walled village in the Geopark, reached by Sunday/holiday ferry from Ma Liu Shui.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Well-preserved village and mangroves.</p><p>niche · Tai Po</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lai%20Chi%20Wo%2C%20Lai%20Chi%20Wo%2C%20Tai%20Po%2C%20New%20Territories%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.210063,22.410401,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cheung Chau</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Car-free island by ferry from Central Pier 5.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Seafood and beaches.</p><p>strong · Cheung Chau</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Cheung%20Chau%2C%20Cheung%20Chau%20Ferry%20Pier%2C%20Cheung%20Chau%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.028342,22.208584,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lamma Island</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Island hike from Yung Shue Wan to Sok Kwu Wan with seafood.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Easy hike.</p><p>strong · Lamma Island</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Lamma%20Island%2C%20Yung%20Shue%20Wan%2C%20Lamma%20Island%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.111712,22.22557,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Peng Chau</name><description><![CDATA[<p>Small car-free island, walkable in about an hour, with a temple by the water (Lung Mo) and the Leather Factory art space on Wing On Street.</p><p><b>Why:</b> Slow village Hong Kong 30-35 min by fast ferry from Central.</p><p>niche · Peng Chau</p><p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Peng%20Chau%2C%20Peng%20Chau%3B%20ferry%20from%20Central%20Pier%206%2C%20Hong%20Kong%2C%20Hong%20Kong%20SAR%2C%20China">Google Maps</a></p>]]></description><Point><coordinates>114.044312,22.285922,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
</Folder>
